<h2>The dispute</h2>
<p>The respondent had issued a work order of May 2021 for concrete batching, mixing, transport and pumping at a medical college project in Hamirpur, valued at about Rs 1.99 crore. The petitioner claimed Rs 19.30 lakh — unpaid dues including a security deposit — with interest, and invoked arbitration by a Section 21 notice in July 2025. The respondent denied the claims and, more fundamentally, denied that any arbitration agreement existed. Everything turned on two clauses of the work order&rsquo;s Annexure III: Clause 18, under which disputes were to be &ldquo;resolved amicably in consultation with Chairman-ACIL&rdquo;, and Clause 19, which conferred exclusive jurisdiction on the courts at Delhi.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of a non-arbitration clause</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>No adjudicatory reference.</strong> The clause did not refer disputes to the Chairman for decision; it contemplated amicable resolution in consultation with him. There was no process for claims, defences or evidence.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>An in-house consultee.</strong> The person to be consulted was the respondent&rsquo;s own Chairman — the hallmark of an internal escalation mechanism, not an independent forum.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Finality language insufficient.</strong> Following the Supreme Court&rsquo;s recent decision in SMS Limited, 2026 (1) SCC 545, &ldquo;final and binding&rdquo; wording alone does not make a clause arbitral.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The neighbouring clause pointed to courts.</strong> Clause 19&rsquo;s exclusive jurisdiction for Delhi courts, read with Clause 18, showed a scheme of amicable resolution followed by litigation — inconsistent with arbitration.</div>
</div>
<p>The Court applied <em>Jagdish Chander v. Ramesh Chander</em>, (2007) 5 SCC 719, and <em>Mahanadi Coalfields</em>, (2022) 20 SCC 636, and distinguished the older line of cases — <em>Rukmanibai Gupta</em> and others — where clauses referred disputes to a named authority <em>for decision</em>. It also reaffirmed that at the Section 11 stage the court&rsquo;s examination is confined to the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement; but that limited examination cannot manufacture an agreement where the text contains none.</p>

<h2>Drafting lessons</h2>
<p>Disputes over whether a clause is an arbitration agreement are among the most avoidable in commercial practice. A clause intended to be arbitral should say so in operative words: disputes <em>shall be referred to arbitration</em>; the tribunal&rsquo;s composition and appointment mechanism; the seat; and the governing rules. Conversely, businesses that intend only an escalation ladder — project manager, then senior management, then litigation — should avoid the word &ldquo;arbitration&rdquo; in headings altogether, because it invites precisely this litigation. Where a counterparty&rsquo;s standard form contains a consultation-style clause, the time to negotiate a real arbitration clause is before signature; after disputes arise, as this case shows, the court will enforce the bargain as written.</p>

<h2>The procedural footnote</h2>
<p>The petitioner had also been through a failed pre-institution mediation on a different project between the same parties — a reminder that under Section 12A of the Commercial Courts Act, 2015, a commercial suit (the petitioner&rsquo;s remaining route) not contemplating urgent interim relief must be preceded by mediation. The dismissal was with liberty to pursue remedies in accordance with law, and the Court expressed no opinion on the merits of the money claims.</p>
<div class="note">Section 7 of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996 defines an arbitration agreement; Section 11(6) empowers the High Court to appoint arbitrators where the agreed procedure fails; Section 21 fixes the commencement of arbitral proceedings by notice. Clause construction is intensely text-specific, and this article is informational only.</div>
